Let \(x=T^{1/(2\beta+1)}\) and \(p_\beta=1/(2\beta+1)\).  Since \(T\ge2\), \(x\ge2^{p_\beta}\), and the choice \(b_T=\lceil x\rceil\) satisfies
\[
 b_T\le x+1\le x\{1+2^{-p_\beta}\}.
\]
Moreover, \(b_T\ge x\), so \((b_T+1)^{-\beta}\le x^{-\beta}\).  Applying \(\mathrm{lem{:}markov\mbox{-}upper\mbox{-}audit}\) at \(b=b_T\) therefore yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
 \sup_{Y\in\mathcal P_{T,\beta}(B,C)}
 \mathbb E_{\pi^{(b_T)},Y}
 [\{\widehat\theta_{b_T}-\theta_T(Y)\}^2]
 &\le
 \left\{
 B\sqrt{(8\mathrm e-4)(1+2^{-p_\beta})}
 +\frac{2\mathrm eC}{\beta}
 \right\}^2
 T^{-2\beta/(2\beta+1)}\\
 &=K_+(B,C,\beta)T^{-2\beta/(2\beta+1)}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The Markov kernel belongs to \(\mathcal D_T\), and \(\widehat\theta_{b_T}\) is observed-data measurable.  Taking the two infima in \(\mathrm{def{:}minimax\mbox{-}mse}\) proves the claimed bound for \(R_T^{(2)}(\beta;B,C)\).

The final sentence has the following precise certificate interpretation.  Among \(b\in\{1,\ldots,T\}\), take the least integer minimizing the explicit right side in \(\mathrm{lem{:}markov\mbox{-}upper\mbox{-}audit}\) (or, equivalently for the stated conclusion, the least integer whose displayed certificate is no larger than the one at \(b_T\)).  Its certified risk is no larger than the certificate at the admissible choice \(b_T\), so the same displayed upper bound follows.
